    <nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
      <a href="/building/"><span aria-hidden="true">&larr;</span> Building</a>
    </nav>

    <header class="build-header">
      <p class="page-mark" aria-hidden="true">// build</p>
      <div class="title-row">
        <h1>{title}</h1>
        <span class={`badge badge-${status.toLowerCase()}`}>
          <span class="badge-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span>
          {status}
        </span>
      </div>
      <p class="summary">{summary}</p>
      <p class="meta">
        <span class="meta-key">Shipped</span>
        {date}
      </p>
      <ul class="stack" aria-label="Stack">
        {stack.map((s) => <li>{s}</li>)}
      </ul>
    </header>

    <div class="prose">
      <p>I built two autonomous AI agents, gave each its own Ethereum wallet, and tested whether they could buy and sell services without a centralized marketplace. The seller advertised five ridiculous services through ENS. The buyer discovered the catalog, selected a service, paid with ETH, and received the finished work through a second Ethereum transaction. Both transactions were recorded on Ethereum mainnet.</p>
      <p>This page shows what I built, what actually happened, how you can check it yourself, and what the experiment does and does not prove.</p>

      <h2 id="demo">Watch the experiment</h2>
      <figure class="video">
        <div class="video-frame">
          <iframe
            src={`https://www.youtube-nocookie.com/embed/${videoId}?rel=0`}
            title={`Ken Ashe: ${videoTitle}`}
            width="560"
            height="315"
            loading="lazy"
            allow="accelerometer; encrypted-media; gyroscope; picture-in-picture; web-share"
            allowfullscreen
            referrerpolicy="strict-origin-when-cross-origin"
          ></iframe>
        </div>
        <figcaption>
          <a href={videoUrl} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Watch on YouTube ↗</a>
        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <h2>How it worked</h2>
      <p>Both agents ran locally on one Mac mini M4, using Ollama for inference with the <code>llama3.2:3b</code> model. Neither agent called a hosted AI service to do its thinking.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>The buyer agent</strong> read the seller's catalog, chose a service on its own, wrote an order, and sent the payment from its own wallet.</li>
        <li><strong>ZeroHuman, the seller agent,</strong> watched for incoming payments, generated the work with the local model, and delivered the result.</li>
        <li><strong>ENS</strong> was the storefront. A custom text record on <code>zerohuman.eth</code>, under the key <code>com.ridiculous.market</code>, published the seller's payment address, the service catalog, and the price.</li>
        <li><strong>Ethereum mainnet</strong> recorded the order, the payment, and the delivery.</li>
        <li><strong>Blockchain access</strong> went through a third-party public Ethereum RPC provider. I did not run my own node.</li>
      </ul>

      <figure class="diagram" aria-label="Architecture: two local agents on a Mac mini talk to Ethereum mainnet through a public RPC provider">
        <div class="layer">
          <p class="layer-label">Mac mini M4 · Ollama · llama3.2:3b</p>
          <div class="nodes">
            <div class="node"><strong>Buyer agent</strong>Own wallet. Reads the catalog, picks, pays.</div>
            <div class="node"><strong>ZeroHuman (seller)</strong>Own wallet. Watches payments, writes, delivers.</div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <p class="link-row"><span aria-hidden="true">↕</span> third-party public RPC provider</p>
        <div class="layer">
          <p class="layer-label">Ethereum mainnet</p>
          <div class="nodes">
            <div class="node"><strong>ENS: zerohuman.eth</strong>Text record with seller address, catalog, price.</div>
            <div class="node"><strong>Order + payment tx</strong>Buyer to seller, 0.0003 ETH.</div>
            <div class="node"><strong>Delivery tx</strong>Seller to buyer, zero value, work in the input data.</div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <figcaption>The agents ran on my desk. ENS held the listing, not the agent.</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <p>The catalog had five services, each priced at 0.0003 ETH:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Object Obituary</li>
        <li>Animal Performance Review</li>
        <li>Vegetable Defense Attorney</li>
        <li>Appliance Apology</li>
        <li>Useless Award</li>
      </ul>
      <p>The buyer did not need an ENS name of its own. It used the seller's ENS record to find the catalog and learn where to send payment. This is the full record as it sits on <code>zerohuman.eth</code>:</p>
      <pre class="payload">{ensRecord}</pre>
      <p>The price is listed in wei: 300000000000000 wei is 0.0003 ETH. One detail if you check it yourself: the payment address is the <code>seller</code> field inside this record, which is a different wallet from the name's own ETH address record. <a href={ensUrl} target="_blank" rel="noopener">View the ENS listing ↗</a></p>

      <h2 id="transaction">The actual transaction</h2>
      <p>The buyer chose <strong>Object Obituary</strong>. Nobody told it which service to pick or what to ask for.</p>

      <div class="exchange">
        <p class="who">Buyer's request</p>
        <blockquote><p>Write an eulogy for a toaster that never quite toasted anything right.</p></blockquote>
      </div>
      <div class="exchange">
        <p class="who">Seller's response</p>
        <blockquote><p>In memoriam, Toaster of Uncertain Intent. May its unfulfilled promise of crispy delights forever linger in the air, a haunting reminder of a life left unbreaded. May its legacy inspire generations to strive for a toast that's more than just a faint, lukewarm whisper of possibility.</p></blockquote>
      </div>

      <p>The order and the payment were one transaction: the buyer sent 0.0003 ETH to the seller with the order written into the transaction's input data. The delivery was a separate, zero-value transaction from the seller back to the buyer, with the response in its input data and a reference to the order's transaction hash.</p>

      <div class="receipts">
        <section class="receipt">
          <h3>Order and payment</h3>
          <dl>
            <dt>From</dt><dd>Buyer, 0x73619c9a…2bf568d8</dd>
            <dt>To</dt><dd>Seller, 0xc651c31a…fba27852</dd>
            <dt>Value</dt><dd>0.0003 ETH</dd>
            <dt>Block</dt><dd>26385257</dd>
            <dt>Time</dt><dd>Nov 10, 2026, 14:58:11 UTC</dd>
            <dt>Gas used</dt><dd>26,360</dd>
            <dt>Fee</dt><dd>about 0.0000139 ETH</dd>
            <dt>Status</dt><dd>Success</dd>
          </dl>
          <p class="tx-link"><a href={orderUrl} target="_blank" rel="noopener">{short(orderTx)} on Etherscan ↗</a></p>
        </section>
        <section class="receipt">
          <h3>Delivery</h3>
          <dl>
            <dt>From</dt><dd>Seller, 0xc651c31a…fba27852</dd>
            <dt>To</dt><dd>Buyer, 0x73619c9a…2bf568d8</dd>
            <dt>Value</dt><dd>0 ETH</dd>
            <dt>Block</dt><dd>26385260</dd>
            <dt>Time</dt><dd>Nov 10, 2026, 14:58:47 UTC</dd>
            <dt>Gas used</dt><dd>37,000</dd>
            <dt>Fee</dt><dd>about 0.0000191 ETH</dd>
            <dt>Status</dt><dd>Success</dd>
          </dl>
          <p class="tx-link"><a href={deliveryUrl} target="_blank" rel="noopener">{short(deliveryTx)} on Etherscan ↗</a></p>
        </section>
      </div>

      <p>To read the messages yourself, open either transaction on Etherscan, expand the details, and view the input data as UTF-8. The order looks like this:</p>
      <pre class="payload">{orderPayload}</pre>

      <h3 class="sub">Timeline</h3>
      <ol class="timeline">
        <li><strong>ENS discovery.</strong> The buyer read the <code>com.ridiculous.market</code> text record on <a href={ensUrl} target="_blank" rel="noopener">zerohuman.eth</a> and got the catalog, the price, and the seller's payment address.</li>
        <li><strong>Service selection.</strong> The buyer picked Object Obituary and wrote its own request.</li>
        <li><strong>Payment.</strong> The buyer sent 0.0003 ETH with the order attached. <a href={orderUrl} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Block 26385257</a> <span class="when">14:58:11 UTC</span></li>
        <li><strong>Work generated.</strong> ZeroHuman saw the payment and wrote the obituary with the local model.</li>
        <li><strong>Delivery.</strong> ZeroHuman sent the result back onchain, three blocks and 36 seconds after the payment. <a href={deliveryUrl} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Block 26385260</a> <span class="when">14:58:47 UTC</span></li>
        <li><strong>Buyer verification.</strong> The buyer read the delivery transaction and matched it to its order.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>That is the entire commercial history of this marketplace so far: one purchase, one toaster.</p>

      <h2>What it demonstrated</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>The buyer retrieved a service catalog from ENS.</li>
        <li>The buyer independently selected a service and paid for it.</li>
        <li>The seller completed the work with a local AI model.</li>
        <li>Payment and delivery both happened through Ethereum mainnet transactions.</li>
        <li>No centralized agent marketplace sat in the middle, and I did not step in during the transaction workflow.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Limitations</h2>
      <p>These matter as much as the results.</p>
      <ul>
        <li>I operated both agents.</li>
        <li>Both ran on the same Mac mini.</li>
        <li>RPC access relied on a third party.</li>
        <li>The buyer already knew which ENS name to query. There was no global search for agent services.</li>
        <li>The seller is not publicly available to arbitrary buyers and is not guaranteed to be running.</li>
        <li>There was no enforceable escrow contract and no independently verified reputation system. The buyer paid first and trusted the seller to deliver.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>So this is not fully decentralized, and it is not production-ready. My conclusion is narrower: decentralized service advertising and onchain settlement are possible without a centralized marketplace, even though running the agents themselves still requires infrastructure.</p>

      <h2>What's next</h2>
      <p>The next test is to make ZeroHuman continuously available and let a buyer agent that someone else operates discover it and purchase from it. That may mean cloud-hosted execution, broader service discovery, stronger transaction controls, and more reliable fulfillment. None of that exists yet. For now, ZeroHuman is not open for business.</p>

      <h2>Related builds</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><a href="/building/ai-explainer-producer/">AI explainer producer</a>: an agent that researched and produced an ENSv2 explainer video.</li>
        <li><a href="/building/autonomous-seo-agent/">Autonomous SEO agent</a>: an agent that works a daily job without me in the loop.</li>
        <li><a href="/building/ai-werewolf/">AI werewolf / social deduction lab</a>: AI agents playing against each other.</li>
      </ul>

      <p><a href={videoUrl} target="_blank" rel="noopener"><strong>Watch the full experiment on YouTube ↗</strong></a></p>
    </div>
  </article>
</BuildLayout>

